<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/appraisal-practice/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal Demand Representation</span></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal service</p><h1>Appraisal Demand Representation for New Jersey Claims</h1><p class="lede">The appraisal clause turns a stalled argument about money into a three-person panel with a deadline. When you invoke it, or your insurer does, we serve as the appraiser on your side of the table.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn amber" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn glass" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Your role</dt><dd>You name us</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Decides</dt><dd>Amount of loss only</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where New Jersey’s appraisal language comes from</h2>
<p>New Jersey adopted a standard fire policy by statute decades ago, and its appraisal paragraph still shapes the homeowners and commercial forms sold here. In plain terms: if you and the insurer fail to agree on the amount of loss, either side may demand appraisal in writing. Each side picks a competent, disinterested appraiser, commonly within 20 days. The two appraisers choose an umpire. If they can’t agree on one, a judge can be asked to appoint one. A written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>Your own policy may change those details. Endorsements, commercial forms and surplus-lines policies often reword the clause, so we read yours before anything else.</p>
<h2>What we do once we’re named</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check the demand and the clock.</strong> We confirm who invoked appraisal, when, and what deadlines now apply to you.</li>
<li><strong>Scope the loss ourselves.</strong> We inspect, measure and photograph the property rather than marking up the carrier’s estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Price it for your county.</strong> Labor and material costs in Bergen County are not the same as in Cumberland County. Our line-item estimate reflects local pricing, New Jersey sales tax on materials and the permit and code items your municipality will actually require.</li>
<li><strong>Work the differences.</strong> We meet the carrier’s appraiser, walk the property together where useful, and narrow the dispute to specific line items.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare for the umpire.</strong> Items still open go to the umpire with photos, measurements and pricing support for each one.</li>
<li><strong>Read the award before you do.</strong> We make sure replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value are stated clearly by coverage.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Know the limits</b>Appraisal sets a number. It does not decide coverage, and it does not award interest, penalties or legal fees. If the insurer has denied the claim outright, a New Jersey attorney is usually the better first call.</div>
<h2>Why independence matters</h2>
<p>The clause asks for a “disinterested” appraiser. If your appraiser has a stake in the repair contract or is paid in a way that looks like a stake in the outcome, the carrier may challenge the award. We disclose our fee terms in writing and we don’t take repair work on the properties we appraise.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can I invoke appraisal myself, or does it have to come from the insurer?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Under most policy language either side can make the written demand. Check whether your policy requires anything first, such as a sworn proof of loss or a written statement of the amount you dispute.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long do I have to name my appraiser in New Jersey?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Policies based on the standard fire policy commonly give each side 20 days from the demand. Your form may differ, so read the appraisal paragraph and any endorsements that change it.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does invoking appraisal stop the time limit for a lawsuit?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Not automatically. Many New Jersey policies limit suits to 12 months from the loss, and courts have allowed that period to pause while a claim is under review. Ask a New Jersey attorney how it applies to your dates.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="related"><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-practice/homeowners-insurance-appraisal/"><small>Service</small>Homeowners Insurance Appraisal<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-lin